---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const ipmCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The economic threshold', note: 'Not "any pest is too many": spray when damage cost exceeds control cost. Below the threshold, spraying loses money AND kills the beneficial insects doing free work — the framework that made pest management economic, not emotional.' },
    { math: '2 · Prevention first', note: 'Crop rotation (breaks pest cycles — corn rootworm starves without corn), resistant varieties (the breeding lesson), planting dates, sanitation. Most pest problems are invitations the field layout sent.' },
    { math: '3 · Biological control', note: 'Predators and parasitoids (ladybirds on aphids, Trichogramma wasps on eggs) — the ecology lesson\'s food webs, employed. Farms that spray less keep their beneficials; the pesticide treadmill kills its own allies.' },
    { math: '4 · Pesticides last, precisely', note: 'When chemicals are needed: right product, right timing (pest\'s vulnerable stage, bees not foraging), rotation of modes of action (resistance management — the evolution lesson running in fast-forward on every spray). IPM integrates, never starts with the spray.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/intermediate/pests-weeds-and-ipm">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Integrated Pest Management is <strong>ecology substituting for calendar spraying</strong>:
    thresholds, prevention, biological control, and chemistry only as the last, best-targeted
    move. It is the agriculture lesson where the ecology ladder pays its rent.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="thresholds, prevention, beneficials, precise chemistry" widget="equationStep" config={ipmCfg} />

  <h2>The resistance treadmill</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pesticide treadmill, documented:</strong> >500 arthropod species now resist at least one chemical; herbicide-resistant weeds (palmer amaranth vs glyphosate) forced the return of tillage on some US farms. Darwin collects his royalty on every overused mode of action (the evolution lesson).</li>
    <li><strong>Refuges and rotations manage resistance:</strong> Bt crops require non-Bt refuges (susceptible mates dilute resistance genes); herbicide rotation mixes selection pressures — resistance management is population genetics, applied on tractors.</li>
    <li><strong>Weeds are the quiet yield thief:</strong> uncontrolled, weeds cost more yield than insects and diseases combined; weed suppression via cover crops (living mulch) is the organic-systems workhorse.</li>
    <li><strong>Pollinators are part of the system:</strong> a third of food crops benefit from insect pollination — IPM\'s bee-safe windows and integrated vegetation management keep the free labour employed (the ecology lesson\'s ecosystem services, contracted).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Stern et al. (1959) — the founding IPM paper; IRAC/FRAC resistance tables; the ecology and evolution lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
